{"page_content": "Determinant Biological sewage treatment plant (STP) Discharge rate of the biological sewage treatment plant (STP) Application to agricultural soil of the sludge of the biological sewage treatment plant (STP) Receiving surface water flow rate Release factors or rates (before biological STP) Default assessment By default, the releases to fresh and marine water are expected to be treated in a standard biological STP for all uses except in case of direct release to agricultural soil. Treatment efficiency and corresponding release factors are calculated by the SIMPLETREAT model, using substance properties and results of screening tests on biotic degradation as an input. See also R.16-2.2.4 and R.16-3. 2 000 m?/day (see also R.16-3) The sludge is by default assumed to be applied to agricultural soil (see R.16-3) 18 000 m?/day (corresponding to a dilution factor of 10). For marine water, the dilution factor is set equal to 100 (see R.16-3). In case of direct releases to agricultural soil the receiving water compartment is a ditch with a residence time of 40 d. ERCs correspond to default release factors (see R.16-2.3) which are listed in see Appendix A.16-1. They are based on the assumption that no environmental risk management is in place. Refined assessment For uses at industrial sites, in known situations, there may be no biological STP or there may be a specific biological STP (with specific discharge rate and specific treatment of the sludge, see parameters below). For a site-specific STP, treatment effectiveness and corresponding release factors may be refined based on measured data. It is also possible to refine the assumed treatment effectiveness in municipal STPs, if substance-specific evidence is available. See also R.16-3.2.2. For site-specific assessment, the flow rate can be changed according to the site-specific data. For site-specific assessments, if incineration or other waste treatment of the sludge is foreseen, then agricultural application of sludge does not take place. For site-specific assessments, the flow rate or the dilution factor can be changed according to the site-specific data. The default release factor can be refined by taking into account i) release preventing techniques or ii) onsite RMM with set effectiveness. SPERCs, developed by industrial sector organisations, are meant to support such refinement. They are supposed to reflect releases based on OC/RMM typically existing at the DU sector level. Other possible sources to refine the", "metadata": {"doc_id": "d36bb990-251d-489e-9eaa-f785d79c4172", "content_type": "Table", "filename": "IR_CSR_R16_V4_FINAL.pdf"}}